{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "﻿Fashion flows through Paris, Milan, London, and \nNew York - and for centuries, these cities have been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4720,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the birthplaces for the latest styles, talent, \nand ideas. As ground zero for some of the world’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9640,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most prestigious brands, it’s in these cities \nwhere trends are born. Yet the biggest fashion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14000,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "companies today are not English, American, \nor Italian, but instead Spanish, Japanese,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Swedish - with annual sales near or exceeding \nluxury powerhouses like Chanel, Gucci, and Hermes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24280,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UNIQLO, H\u0026M, and Zara emerged in the 1990s and \nhave dominated the mass market for the past three  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decades. Fast fashion, for better or worse, has \nwithstood the test of time. These upstarts rapidly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanded across the West in the 2010s - taking \nmarket share from giants like GAP and Ralph Lauren  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40200,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on their home turf, driving J Crew to bankruptcy, \nand turning Abercrombie and American Eagle into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45280,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "afterthoughts. When the industry realized they \ncouldn’t beat fast fashion, they joined them. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49680,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The game has changed. A space that historically \ncompeted on creativity and creation has now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pivoted towards speed and curation. Fast fashion \noptimizes for speed and cost over quality. Why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60200,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gamble on innovation when you can profit from \nproven demand? They watch what’s popular and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64640,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then create products to match the latest trend as \nquickly as possible so they can sell while demand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exists. They manufacture in small quantities to \nminimize inventory. They sell cheap and produce  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74280,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even cheaper by outsourcing and using mostly synthetic \nmaterials. The playbook is simple - get in,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get out, and then reposition for the next trend. \nWhat fast fashion labels lose in quality and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84400,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "originality, they make up for in value and \nvolume. The mass market has embraced their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88760,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "promise of endless variety and affordability. \nModern clothing is no longer made to last - and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93440,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consumers don’t mind because they can easily \nreplace what breaks with what’s new at the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97680,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "low cost. Despite the shorter lifespan per piece, \npeople are buying more clothes than ever these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "days. Even the fashion houses and legacy labels \nwho built their empires on selling timeless,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107440,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buy-for-life clothing have transformed \nthemselves over the years to be more like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110960,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UNIQLO, H\u0026M, and ZARA. They’ve copied their \nbusiness practices, poached their talent,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115480,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and shifted their catalogs to sell more synthetic, \nlimited-run products in the pursuit of growth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120240,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While people tend to paint UNIQLO, ZARA, and \nH\u0026M under the broad stroke of fast fashion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124840,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in reality, they all operate very differently. On \npaper, anyone should be able to replicate their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "playbook. Factories can be easily Googled and \ntrends can be seen in minutes on social media.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134720,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Above all, fashion is fickle. In this world, \nsuccess is difficult to achieve and even harder  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139200,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to maintain. The industry is littered \nwith the bodies of brands like Burberry,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143160,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "American Apparel, and Under \nArmour who all once looked unstoppable but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ended up as being just a flash-in-the-pan. To get \nto the top is one feat. To stay on the throne is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "another. As everyone else has started copying \nwhat they’re doing, Uniqlo, ZARA, and H\u0026M have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weathered the storm to varying degrees of success. \nFast fashion is a case study on evolution as much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162400,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as it is about moats. In business, how you \nview competition is the difference between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166680,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fleeting success and sustained success. In \nthis episode, we’re diving into how UNIQLO,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171360,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "H\u0026M, and ZARA conquered the fashion industry \nin 3 decades, the strategies that have brought  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175840,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them to where they are today, and exploring what \ncould one day topple these modern-day empires."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269720,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the simplest form, every business can \nbe broken into 3 life stages - surviving,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scaling, and sustaining. Any company at any time \nis in one of these buckets. UNIQLO started in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279480,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1980s as a chain of stores selling cheap clothes \nto the frugal Japanese masses. The company was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284560,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more of a real estate play than a fashion play. \nIn this era before e-commerce, they achieved  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289160,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "product-market-fit by blanketing Japan’s suburbs \nwith stores. Just like the fashion giants of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293920,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "West, UNIQLO competed on availability rather \nthan product in this era before e-commerce.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299080,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The clothes they carried came straight from \nChinese factories with minimal input. Their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "competitors were not established labels, but \ninstead local boutiques and large-scale discount  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307080,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chains. Anyone selling cheap clothes nearby \nwas a threat to UNIQLO. A better-funded competitor could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always come in stocking the same products \nat similar price points. UNIQLO’s founder,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316560,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tadashi Yanai, then 40 years old, realized \nthat they needed to become a brand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320840,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than just a set of stores with clean floors \nand decent service. He wanted UNIQLO to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "become for Japan what GAP was in the \nU.S. and Marks \u0026 Spencer was in the U.K."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330240,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While Tadashi had the ambition to \nturn UNIQLO into a global brand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he was measured in execution. Japan’s most \nvaluable export is not cars or circuit boards,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338480,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but instead culture. This bet would pay off in spades \nwith not just the conventionally nationalist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Japanese, but also later with the Western markets that have \nromanticized Japan for generations. Between 1990  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348920,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to 2004, UNIQLO was in its first era of survival, \ntaking steps to transform itself into a “by Japan,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355120,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for Japan” national brand. Tadashi laid \nout a revenue prerequisite of a billion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358760,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "US dollars in domestic sales. Achieving this \nsales target in his mind would signify that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UNIQLO had successfully established itself as \nJapan's top brand and would then be ready to compete  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368040,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the global stage. To do that, UNIQLO needed to \nstart designing its own clothes rather than simply  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372720,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buying from factory catalogs. Yet the business \nplan had to remain the same - to sell everyday,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "practical clothing at the lowest market price. \nAll of this unfolded against the backdrop of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381960,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Japan's lost decade. The 1990s was a harsh \ntime for the country with the stock market crash,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deflation, and high unemployment. UNIQLO needed \ndifferentiation to grow sales at home,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391520,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also needed to find ways to keep prices low under \nthis decade-long recession. Since most of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396360,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capital was tied into stores, there \njust wasn’t enough money left to risk on bold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400040,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "designs - and there was no way UNIQLO would \never come close to the creativity of big brands  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and fashion houses. Out of necessity, the company \nappealed to the modest, pragmatic Japanese mass market by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409920,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "staying far away from the loud branding \nthat had become prominent in American fashion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414200,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was in the late 1990s when Tadashi found the \nwinning formula. Fleeces had become the hottest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418800,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing in America and that trend had flowed to \nJapan. Yet the garment was out of reach for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422800,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the average Japanese citizen. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UNIQLO tapped into domestic demand by rolling out \na low-cost yet differentiated synthetic fleece.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429560,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To stand out from the inevitable flood of cheap \nfleeces, they had proactively tweaked the design,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433840,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "used a unique blend of synthetic fibers, and marketed that \nstronger insulation and improved practicality.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438760,
    "dDurationMs": 5347,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was UNIQLO's first major product to not just be cheap, but actually quality and stylish in its own right. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444107,
    "dDurationMs": 4213,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sales doubled in a single year and \nthen quadrupled the year after. Tadashi realized  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448320,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that UNIQLO could lift-and-shift this formula \nto fill these “latent consumer needs”. Identify  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453880,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trending pieces that were too expensive for the \naverage Japanese, replace their natural fibers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with synthetic for lower cost, but proactively make enough tweaks to  “exceed customer expectations” through merit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464720,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UNIQLO surpassed $1B in 2000 thanks to the success of their fleece. The company followed through its commitment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471320,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by opening up the first international UNIQLO \nstore in London a year later. But by 2003,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475680,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fleeces had fallen out of fashion and sales \nplummeted - showing Tadashi that this formula  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479880,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would only work with speed. You can strike gold \nbut one product will never be enough to sustain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484640,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "growth. Since UNIQLO was losing millions overseas \non new stores across the West thousands of miles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489800,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away from home, they needed to move faster than \never to survive with these sunk costs - especially  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the face of declining profits. Overseas sales \nwere sluggish as no one was getting excited about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499040,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a cheap clothing brand from Japan. In search of \nhits, UNIQLO accelerated product cycles from 2  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to 6 times a year and signed a 5-year, $1 billion \ndollar deal with chemical suppliers to develop new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509080,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "synthetic fibers. Material innovation would enable \nUNIQLO to compete on merit rather than cost alone.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514240,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UNIQLO needed to shed its reputation from being \ngood for the price to being good in general. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518760,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was a page out of the sportswear industry. Beyond \nlogos, Adidas, Nike, and Under Armour differentiate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their products through proprietary materials \nthat they aggressively brand like Flyknit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528480,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and ClimaCool. Inventory turnover measures how \nmany times a company sells out its inventory in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a single year and Uniqlo cycled through its inventory \nalmost 9 times in a single year in search of hits.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538600,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By 2004, UNIQLO had rolled out cashmere sweaters \npriced 50% cheaper than the market, HEATTECH  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "winter wear, and embedded workers at factories \nto oversee production with the new materials."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548880,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In-house synthetics became the moat, Japanese \nculture remained the hook, and affordability  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was the closer. These measures reversed the dip \nand returned UNIQLO to a positive trajectory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From 2005 to 2015, Uniqlo entered its second \nstage of scaling. Wherever the trends went,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563520,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they followed. When the market gravitated towards \nskinny jeans, UNIQLO responded with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567600,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultra-Stretch and a denim collection. When \npuffers and parkas became fashionable, they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571680,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rolled out Ultra Light Down and when business casual became in-fashion, they rolled out chinos. When athleisure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577960,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turned into a sensation, they created AIRISM and \nwhen collaborations exploded across the industry, they launched collections  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with big-name designers like Christopher Lemaire \nand celebrities like Pharrel. When streetwear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "became the latest fad, UNIQLO poached the \nfounder of BAPE to lead design. UNIQLO made  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592560,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a splash in every new market, planting flagship \nstores in the most expensive cities. A big, fancy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "store also carried value as a marketing asset as\nthese stores were built to convey quality and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602360,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demonstrate that UNIQLO belonged on the same block \nas any big fashion brand - all of which means less money  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607200,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needed for advertising. UNIQLO’s domination in \nJapan was what funded their expansion across  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612120,
    "dDurationMs": 6372,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the West. But it would ultimately take 8 years before UNIQLO would turn a profit abroad. This runway was only possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618492,
    "dDurationMs": 3998,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the strength of Japan. This is where prior \nbrands like Abercrombie had failed -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622490,
    "dDurationMs": 7677,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they had also opened up these dazzling, flagship properties overseas. But when domestic sales began to dip, they opted to double down on expansion,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630167,
    "dDurationMs": 7753,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hoping that new growth abroad would be enough to offset the decline at home. By the 2010s, major Western brands were ramping up their investment in Asia  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under this sink-or-swim mentality and Tadashi took \na defensive posture. In response, UNIQLO blanketed Japan with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tens of net new stores year after year through the \nmid-2010s. Surprisingly, there were no signs of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fatigue or saturation. The Japanese remained fiercely \nloyal as the average UNIQLO store in Japan  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654400,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consistently grossed nearly twice as much as the \naverage UNIQLO store abroad. This is all the more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658880,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impressive when we consider the higher prices \noverseas, the continued stagnation of Japan,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663360,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the strength of the euro and dollar relative \nto the yen. But Tadashi was still paranoid. He saw  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668440,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on his visits abroad that there still existed a \nmarket where companies like Target and Primark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673640,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were able to thrive selling bottom of the barrel basic clothes. Not everyone had graduated to fast fashion. To hedge,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679120,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he rolled out GU - a separate, ultra-low-cost \nbrand selling clothes at half the price of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683560,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UNIQLO. And while most brands struggled during \nthe Great Recession, it was in this period when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687640,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UNIQLO took off. International sales surged as \nAmericans flocked to the UNIQLO for affordable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692400,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "essentials - just like the Japanese had \ndone a decade ago during their downturn."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696240,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But UNIQLO progressively found itself unable to match its closest rivals in H\u0026M and ZARA when it came to speed and styles. They could not R\u0026D  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fast enough to sell the latest runway looks and viral outfits. \nRather than speeding up, UNIQLO slowed down.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709040,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sales had grown in a decade from $2B dollars \nin 2005 to $11B dollars in 2015 off of selling everyday  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "basics. The company had built this business through \nrefinement, not pioneering. By the mid-2010s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721160,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UNIQLO had nearly the same amount of stores \nabroad as they did in Japan and the brand at this point was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725840,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well established. Customers knew what to \ngo to UNIQLO for and they needed to be able to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729760,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get the products they were looking for. The \nconventional fast fashion tactic of stocking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733960,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less and selling every possible trend was no longer useful \nat this scale. Inventory turnover dropped from 9  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739360,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the early 2000s to just 3 by 2015 as the company pivoted \nto prioritize availability and optimization. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746560,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flagship stores were expensive - and the company \nwas opening hundreds every year. Despite the rapid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750880,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "growth in sales, UNIQLO’s profits have steadily \ndeclined to as low as they were in the 1990s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756280,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In business, what you measure is what you get. \nTadashi publicly compares UNIQLO’s sales to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760720,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the competition every year in his letter to \nshareholders. He annually updates UNIQLO’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "position and charts his rival’s own performances\nby name and currency. This is in stark contrast to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Western companies, who only address the existence \nof competition after poor results and will go to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775400,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "great lengths to avoid naming their closest \nrivals, even if everyone knows who they’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779240,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talking about. UNIQLO is now in its present-day\nstage of sustaining where priority now is profit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784160,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "optimization. While they still maintain the \nfast fashion practices in frequent stocking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788440,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and multiple deliveries to stores a week, \nthe rotations are more colors and designs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792480,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than products. They still occasionally \ndevelop new trending pieces like rainjackets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796600,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or shoulder bags - but the R\u0026D that they do now is nowhere near \ntheir historical pace. UNIQLO’s current inventory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turnover is lower than that of GAP, Nike, and \nLululemon. The number of stores abroad has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807200,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grown rapidly from 800 to 1,800 in 9 years. While \nthe number of UNIQLO Japan stores has shrunk since  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813160,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2014, the overall domestic business has not \nslowed. The average store in Japan still grosses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818240,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more in a single year than the average store \nabroad, which is wild to consider when we factor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the present-day weakness of the Japanese yen. But when \nwe look beyond earnings productivity, the money  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is actually coming from UNIQLO International - \nas overseas sales these days generate nearly twice  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as much as domestic. With an established growth \nengine and mature product mix, the company has had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837200,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the luxury to shift their focus away from chasing trends to solving internal pain points. UNIQLO has pushed RFIDs and their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843840,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own custom self-checkout machines as a way of permanent \nsolving the labor shortages and addressing Japan’s shrinking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849640,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "population. Profits have improved from the \nmid-2010s for both divisions - with International  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854240,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "making the greatest strides. But the driver of these improvements is not really self-checkout, but rather lower product  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "costs. UNIQLO’s gross margins are currently \nat record-highs. In comparison, margins were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the lowest in the late 90s and 2000s when the \ncompany was aggressively investing in materials  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and products to prove their quality to the market. While this could just be correlation and not causation, it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems more likely that UNIQLO is now downgrading their \nmaterial and quality as a means to secure profits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In business, every decision has its ramifications. This urgency for profits are the results of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "roosters coming home to roost for Tadashi. While \nhe was successful at turning UNIQLO from a random  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889480,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suburban clothing store into a global fashion \npowerhouse, his arguably greatest and only blemish  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894560,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a leader is in M\u0026A. He had dreamed for \ndecades of having a diversified, cross-category,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899800,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "portfolio company like GAP, LVMH, or Kering with \nmultiple underlying billions-dollar brands and UNIQLO just being one of them. Yet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906760,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his acquisitions have never lived up to their \nbilling. There was the American brand Theory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910680,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which UNIQLO purchased back in 2004 for \n$100M, then $290M in 2012 for J Brand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917280,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "75M euros in 2006 for a French label Comptoir, \n$150M for a lingerie company called Princess  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924400,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tam Tam in 2005, then 240M euros in 2009 \nfor an American-Austrian luxury brand by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930560,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the name of Helmut Lang. Two decades later, \nthese brands combined contribute just 15% of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935320,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "overall sales despite consistently having more stores than UNIQLO Japan and UNIQLO  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940000,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "International. Tadashi had spent nearly a billion \ndollars pre-inflation on buying these brands  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944760,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and unknown millions trying to grow \nthem. But their weak performances drag down overall  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948680,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "margins - and if these two decades of investment \nhasn’t helped, it’s hard to ask for more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953440,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patience - which puts it it all on UNIQLO to keep \nprofits and dividends flowing to shareholders."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While UNIQLO was in survival mode during the \n1990s-2000s, H\u0026M in that same period was scaling.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964280,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fast fashion supply chain that Tadashi had \nto build one piece at a time had already been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968400,
    "dDurationMs": 9840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perfected by the Swedish brand. At a time when UNIQLO was still purchasing clothes off of factory catalogs, H\u0026M already had offices around Europe and Asia to be closer to factories,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "employed buyers to build up an internal Rolodex \nof suppliers per region, and invested in their own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983200,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "labs to engineer cheaper synthetic material. \nAll of these measures for H\u0026M had started in the 1970s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989160,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and were done with the goal of being able to source \nthe widest variety of clothes in the shortest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amount of time at the lowest cost. Through the 90s \nto the 2000s, it was clear these investments were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paying off as H\u0026M featured some of the highest \ngross profit of any label in the industry. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003280,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sold hundreds of millions of items every year \nwith the widest catalog of outfits, classics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "basics, sportswear, accessories, and cosmetics. \nTheir leaders kept no secrets about how they saw  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013400,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the world - fashion is perishable and people \nwant to be part of the trends. You either have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017880,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it or you don’t - and for the mass majority of \nconsumers who were priced out, H\u0026M would always  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be there with a budget solution. H\u0026M opted to \ncompete on information and speed over product,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027520,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boasting every year just how in tune they were \nwith consumers. International sales  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031520,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at this point had been the core of H\u0026M’s business \nfor decades. Sweden and the surrounding Nordic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "region represented at most 20% and that sales \ncontribution only shrunk in subsequent years.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The U.S., the UK, Germany, and France were \nH\u0026M’s strongest markets. But while UNIQLO  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047280,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was taking its time to prove its business overseas, H\u0026M \nwas putting pedal to the medal, opening up hundreds of net  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052680,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new stores in every region. They followed \nthe industry-standard playbook by leading  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with big flagships and then fast-following with \nsmaller locations in nearby malls. By the late 2000s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the average H\u0026M store grossed nearly twice as much \nas the average UNIQLO store. Consumers were blown  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066840,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away by H\u0026M’s unprecedented value and variety - \nand sales and profits swelled to record-highs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the 2010s, H\u0026M entered its third life stage of sustaining - \neven though the company mistakenly believed it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was still scaling. H\u0026M at this stage was led \nby Karl-Johan Persson, who had been handed the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keys to the kingdom by his father, who himself was \nhanded the reins by his father, Erling. Karl-John  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087440,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was the third generation in the family to run \nH\u0026M. But because his grandfather had ensured  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1091800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "H\u0026M’s survival in the 50s and his father had \nscaled H\u0026M through the 90s into a global brand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1096760,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Karl-Johan in comparison had it easy. He was born \ninto a family of billionaires and he objectively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102000,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lacked the innate paranoia that plague self-made \nbusinessmen like UNIQLO’s Tadashi, who grew  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up in rural Japan as the son of a roadside tailor. \nWhen Karl-John was appointed CEO of H\u0026M in 2009,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1113480,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he had the luxury and capital to pursue any direction he wanted. \nSurvival and scaling had already been handled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1119200,
    "dDurationMs": 4873,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the prior generations. On the product side, he stuck to the same tactics - when athleisure took off,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124080,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they launched H\u0026M Sport. When collabs became the \nthing, the company brought in Alexander Wang and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1129240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "David Beckham. On the side, he experimented moving H\u0026M \nup-market by hosting fashion shows in Paris,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1134600,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rolled out rewards programs, and supported \nonline ordering when ecommerce took off.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1138880,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But at the 10,000 foot level as a CEO, these \nare merely tactics - not strategy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1143720,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rather than setting a sales goal, Karl-Johan set \nthe number of stores as H\u0026M’s multi-year target.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148160,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He publicly committed to opening 10-15% new stores \nevery year for the next decade. By 2014, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154120,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "company was opening a new store somewhere in the \nworld every single day of the year - a pace never  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158280,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seen before in the industry. As they prioritized \nscale, the company got even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1162000,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "further away from product. Karl-Johan was in his mid-30s and without paranoia and expertise, he could not see the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1168040,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "market changing around them. He could only see \nwhat was happening at the surface-level. He had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1169880,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been born in peacetime and could recognize that \nH\u0026M had a target on its back. When the media and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1174800,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "millennials turned up the heat on fast fashion \nfor its impact on workers and the environment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1179000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was H\u0026M and not UNIQLO, that took the brunt of \nthe criticism. Karl-Johan responded directly to these challenges face-on by leading a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1185560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multi-year charge on ethics and sustainability, \ncommitting fair wages for every factory worker,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1190120,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "auditing suppliers for better working conditions, \nlobbying governments for stronger labor standards,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1194880,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and reducing carbon emissions. The company’s \ndirection boiled down into just these 2  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1198800,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "line-items - sustainability and scale. But \nwhat Karl-Johan couldn't see was that the foundation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1203240,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for H\u0026M’s success was under threat. If \nwe were to abstract clothing as a whole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1207560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it would look like a triangle. At the bottom of the triangle, you have everyday basics are at the bottom with the highest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1212680,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "profits and greatest total addressable \nmarket. These are products that can be ordered in large  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1217040,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "volumes and have demand year-round. Then \nin the middle you have current fashion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1220920,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which are items that customers are seeking in \nthe current season like puffers in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1224120,
    "dDurationMs": 10000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the winter and dresses in the summer. These products in the middle can be sold for a lot more but they also have to be moved before the season ends.  And at the tip of the triangle, you have the trendiest garments which are really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1234120,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only sought after fashion-forward big city dwellers, \nwhich represent the smallest addressable market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1239520,
    "dDurationMs": 10240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and smallest volumes.  But the tradeoff with smaller volumes means the highest prices because you're offering what few people have.  H\u0026M had been successful over the decades offering everything on this triangle at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1249760,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lower cost. Customers would stop in to look at a \ntrendy piece but would end up with some seasonal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1254000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "garments and a few basics or vice versa. But \ndespite the flashy runway pieces and glamor shots,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1258960,
    "dDurationMs": 3663,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "H\u0026M’s bread-and-butter as a business was \nultimately still built on the basics and at the bottom of the triangle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1262704,
    "dDurationMs": 11416,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There were 2 shifts in the mid-2010s.  UNIQLO had specialized in the basics and was now surging.  They were winning customers over at the bottom of the triangle. And at the tip of the triangle you had ZARA,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1274120,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who had specialized in the trendiest pieces and they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1276440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were far more aggressive about rotating inventory \nto win over the higher income, fashion-forward, big city dwellers. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1282440,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left H\u0026M with just the middle of the triangle with current fashion, but even that was progressively contested by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1288080,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ZARA and UNIQLO. Traditional labels like GAP, \nAbercrombie, and Ralph Lauren who had gotten  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1292920,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their lunch eaten for the past decade, were \nnow incorporating fast fashion practices into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1297040,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their seasonal pieces. H\u0026M was effectively being \nsqueezed out. They had thrived in the past  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1301720,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with their first mover advantage as the pioneer \nof fast fashion. But now, they lacked the quality  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1304320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and speed to compete anywhere in the triangle \nas a jack-of-all-trades and a master-of-none."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1309360,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Revenue is a lagging indicator - not a leading \none. By the time Karl-Johan could see the gaps,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1314120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the damage was already done. Overall sales grew as \npeople were still buying H\u0026M clothes - but not at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1319400,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "full price. This was the “relative satisfaction” \nthat Tadashi had been worried about a decade ago for UNIQLO,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1326120,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where customers only develop loyalty to \nthe lowest bidder. By the late 2010s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1331200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "H\u0026M profits had plummeted to record lows. This decline \nwas exacerbated by the fact that H\u0026M had also lost its grip  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1336800,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on its strongest market. Sales in Europe were now regressing \nand the new markets that they were banking on were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1342280,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not performing any better. The only region \nthat was really growing was in the Americas  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1346560,
    "dDurationMs": 11320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the bottom-line was wrecked from operating \nso many stores across two continents. It became impossible to tell if H\u0026M sales were really growing or if they were just enjoying this initial honeymoon injection from these new stores.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1357880,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Inventory turnover dropped from 5 to 2. H\u0026M found itself \ntrapped in a never-ending cycle where they introduced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1363200,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new products every months but they couldn’t sell at full \nprice and they were forced to discount  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1367360,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them just so their stores would have room for the next batch. When faced with declining  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1371520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "numbers, Karl-Johan stubbornly stuck to scaling. \nHe was convinced that this problem was not unique  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1376560,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to H\u0026M, that every fashion brand was suffering from the \nretail apocalypse, and that personalized emails,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1382040,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mobile app experiences, and next-day delivery \nwould turn things around. Karl-Johan was forced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1386160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out in 2019 and by the time he stepped down, \nthere were more H\u0026M stores worldwide than there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1392000,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are UNIQLO and ZARA stores today. H\u0026M today is \nstill stuck in sustaining. For the first time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1397880,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they’ve handed the reins outside the family - \nbut when you hire from the same management tree,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1402360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all you really change are the faces. They appointed \nKarl-Johan’s lieutenant to run the company  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1407000,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but her only achievements before becoming CEO \nwas leading H\u0026M’s superficial sustainability  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1412080,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "programs. Sales plateaued under her tenure and she \nlasted just 4 years before resigning, citing that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1417320,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the job had become too stressful. What the company \nneeds now is a true outsider who has no attachment to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1422400,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the past and has the ruthlessness to challenge what exists as H\u0026M today still operates more stores than GAP and UNIQLO."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1429400,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ZARA is arguably the most secretive company ever \ncovered on Modern MBA. They publish only the bare  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1434400,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minimum required by law. Their leaders and executives stay \nin the shadows and rarely appear publicly or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1439720,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give interviews. Their rationale and worldview has \nremained undisclosed for decades. Their financials  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1444800,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tell us the how and what but leaves us to imagine \nthe why - and all we have are lagging indicators,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1449800,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not leading ones. H\u0026M, UNIQLO, and every other \nlabel source product from third-parties in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1455280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Asia where labor is the cheapest - and they \nwillingly put up with the long lead times  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1459200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and timezones all to save a buck. ZARA does the \nopposite where they run their own factories and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1464240,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work primarily with third-parties that are \nlocated in close proximity to their Spanish  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1468480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "headquarters. By manufacturing and contracting \nacross Morocco, Portugal, Turkey, and Spain,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1473840,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ZARA pays more for product but gains an edge in \nspeed. They turn ideas into finished products  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1479080,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in as fast as 2 weeks and deliver to stores by 3 \n- while UNIQLO is reportedly still stuck at 4-6.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1485400,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ZARA gets flexibility as all the inventory \nflows through Spain under one roof before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1489880,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being parceled out to stores worldwide, \nproduction can start and stop on the fly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1493960,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and there are no minimum order quantities - \nwith everyone working in the same timezone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1497960,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Proximity manufacturing is ultimately only \nuseful if you’re producing many different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1501800,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "products at a time - and this is where ZARA \nexcels. They carry the trendiest pieces and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1506200,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fill the demand for fashion-forward millennials \nwith aspirations for high fashion but lack the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1511040,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "budget for it. ZARA breaks from convention \nwhere the bread-and-butter is meant to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1514400,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in easy-to-manufacture, trend-resistant, everyday \nbasics. They refresh products monthly and produce  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1522440,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in small batches to minimize risk and drive \nurgency. If customers see something they like,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1527280,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they learn to buy quickly as it likely won’t be \naround in a few weeks. Scarcity and exclusivity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1532600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are the drivers, not reliability and availability. \nZARA has carved out their own niche as a provider  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1538200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of high fashion without the steep price tag. \nZARA’s parent company, Inditex is the highest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1542680,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grossing mass-market clothing brand in the \nworld with revenue that lags only behind the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1546680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "luxury conglomerates. While UNIQLO and H\u0026M open \nhundreds of stores per year, ZARA’s expansion has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1552120,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been far slower. They strategically insert their \nstores in luxury areas with the goal of being as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1556720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "close to Gucci, Prada, and LV as possible. Any \nshopper that’s priced out of high fashion will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1561520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "find what they’re looking for at ZARA. They have \nless stores - but every ZARA store grosses far  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1566720,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than those of H\u0026M and UNIQLO. By selling the \nhottest pieces when interest is the highest but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1571880,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "availability is the lowest, ZARA gets to charge \na premium, all of which in turn translates to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1576400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consistent double-digit profits. While fashion \nlabels generally spotlight their designers, ZARA’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1581520,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "designers - like the rest of the company - remain \nhidden. In some ways, the company’s secretive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1585760,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nature is their cost of doing business. They’ll \ndo anything to sell clothes, even if that means  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1590200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "breaking the law. Whether that’s artists, boutique \nbrands, or established luxury players, ZARA has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1595080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been the target of more infringement lawsuits \nand complaints than anyone else in the industry.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1599400,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the plaintiffs have all denounced Inditex \nas a shameless dealer of high-end knockoffs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1604080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "none of this litigation and PR has ever put a dent \nin ZARA’s momentum. The probability that their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1609440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "customers will upgrade permanently to high fashion \nis near-zero. If they do splurge, it would only be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1614320,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a few items. At the end of the day, there’s no \nother brand that’s entrenched in this gap between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1623040,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fast fashion and high fashion and every wardrobe \nmust be balanced out with economical options."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1625600,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the traditional brands have reclaimed \nsome of their market share from H\u0026M - UNIQLO  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1629200,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and ZARA are too entrenched to be toppled by a \nnew player. If mass-market fashion is to evolve,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1633640,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the reality is that that change will not come \nfrom the supply-side. It won’t be some new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1637440,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "business model that topples fast fashion but \nrather a shift in consumers mindset. What’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1641720,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interesting is that it’s not just UNIQLO who has \nstruggled to create a portfolio of hit brands,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1646160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also H\u0026M and Inditex. The market, for whatever \nreason, only really has the appetite for one fast  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1652320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fashion brand at each level of the triangle. \nThere’s emergent behavior that suggests that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1656800,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the market is slowly changing - the revival of \nvintage stores as customers pursue more lasting,  "
    } ]
  }, {
